2. Required Software and AI Model

Before starting the configuration process, all required components must be downloaded and prepared.

2.1 Ollama

Ollama is responsible for downloading, managing, loading, and running AI models locally.

It provides the underlying inference environment that allows Qwen3-Coder to operate on the local computer instead of relying entirely on a remote AI service.

Its primary responsibilities include:

* Downloading and installing the selected AI model.
* Managing local AI model files.
* Loading the model into system memory when required.
* Providing a local API endpoint for other applications.
* Processing requests sent by external applications such as Cline.
* Returning AI-generated responses and code.

Official website: https://ollama.com/⁠￼

2.2 Cline

Cline is the bridge between the AI model and the development environment.

It connects to Ollama and allows the selected AI model to participate in actual programming workflows.

Its capabilities include:

* Understanding natural-language programming instructions.
* Analyzing existing source-code files.
* Generating new code and modifying existing code.
* Identifying potential errors and suggesting solutions.
* Creating and organizing project files.
* Executing terminal commands with appropriate user approval.
* Assisting with debugging, refactoring, and software development.

Official website: https://cline.bot/⁠￼

2.3 Visual Studio Code

Visual Studio Code is the primary graphical development environment used throughout this project.

It provides the interface through which users interact with their code, manage projects, install extensions, and operate Cline.

Its primary responsibilities include:

* Providing a comprehensive source-code editing environment.
* Managing project folders and files.
* Supporting multiple programming languages.
* Installing and managing extensions.
* Hosting the Cline extension.
* Displaying AI-generated code and development suggestions.

Official website: https://code.visualstudio.com/⁠￼

2.4 Qwen3-Coder

Qwen3-Coder is the AI coding model selected as the intelligence engine of this configuration.

Unlike traditional code editors, which primarily provide tools for manually writing and editing source code, an AI coding model can interpret programming instructions and generate code based on the user’s requirements.

Its responsibilities include:

* Understanding programming requirements.
* Generating source code.
* Analyzing existing code structures.
* Finding and explaining programming errors.
* Suggesting optimizations.
* Assisting with project architecture.
* Supporting multi-file programming tasks when used through a compatible coding agent.

The model must be compatible with the installed Ollama version and the available hardware.

Official model library: https://ollama.com/library⁠￼

3. Installation and Configuration Procedure

The entire setup process consists of four major stages.

Installation workflow

* Download the AI Model Package
    Obtain the Qwen3-Coder model through Ollama’s model library or an appropriate supported model distribution.
* Integrate the Model into Ollama
    Install Ollama, download or import the model, and verify that it can run successfully on the local computer.
* Connect Ollama to Cline
    Configure Cline to use Ollama as its model provider, specify the correct model identifier, and establish the local API connection.
* Integrate Cline into Visual Studio Code
    Install the Cline extension in VS Code, configure its provider settings, and verify that the AI coding agent can communicate with the local model.

Step 1: Download the AI Model Package

The first step is to obtain the Qwen3-Coder model package.

Visit the official Ollama model library and locate a Qwen3-Coder version compatible with your hardware and Ollama installation.

Download the model using Ollama’s model management interface or its command-line interface.

For example:

ollama pull qwen3-coder

Note: The exact model name and available tags may vary. Use the actual model identifier listed in Ollama’s library.

During this stage, make sure that:

* The model download completes successfully.
* The model files are stored correctly.
* Sufficient disk space is available.
* The selected model size is appropriate for the computer’s available memory.
* The model is compatible with the intended inference environment.

Step 2: Integrate the AI Model into Ollama

After downloading the model package, the next step is to integrate it into Ollama.

Ollama acts as the local model execution platform. It manages the model and provides the necessary infrastructure for other applications to communicate with it.

Open a terminal and execute:

ollama list

This command displays the AI models currently available in the local Ollama environment.

Next, start the selected model:

ollama run qwen3-coder

If the model starts successfully and responds to a test prompt, the integration process can be considered successful.

Ollama also provides a local API service, commonly accessible through:

http://localhost:11434

This API endpoint is particularly important because it allows Cline to communicate with the AI model without requiring a conventional cloud-based model service.

Step 3: Connect Ollama to Cline

Once Qwen3-Coder is running correctly through Ollama, the next step is to establish a connection between Ollama and Cline.

Cline serves as the intermediary between the local AI model and the user’s programming tasks.

Open Cline’s settings and configure the model provider.

The typical configuration includes:

Configuration	Value
API Provider	Ollama
Base URL	http://localhost:11434
Model	Installed Qwen3-Coder model identifier
API Key	Usually not required for a local Ollama connection

The exact setting names may vary depending on the Cline version.

After configuring the connection, send a simple programming instruction through Cline.

For example:

Create a Python program that prints
"Hello, World!" to the terminal.

If Cline successfully receives a response from Qwen3-Coder, the connection between the two applications has been established.

Step 4: Integrate Cline into Visual Studio Code

The final stage is to integrate Cline into Visual Studio Code.

First, download and install Visual Studio Code if it is not already installed.

Open VS Code, navigate to the Extensions marketplace, search for Cline, and install the official extension.

After installation:

* Open the Cline interface from the VS Code sidebar.
* Access the Cline configuration settings.
* Select Ollama as the AI model provider.
* Confirm the local API address.
* Select the installed Qwen3-Coder model.
* Save the configuration.
* Send a test programming instruction through Cline.

Once the configuration is complete, Cline should be able to communicate with Ollama and use Qwen3-Coder to assist with programming directly inside VS Code.

