3S + 1M = Local AI Coding Environment

Three software applications. One AI coding model. One integrated development workflow.

6. Key Understanding Questions and Answers

To verify that the installation and architecture are fully understood, consider the following questions:

Q1. Why are three software applications required?

A: Because each application performs a different function: Ollama manages and runs the model, Cline handles AI-assisted programming tasks, and VS Code provides the development interface.

Q2. What is the role of Qwen3-Coder?

A: It is the AI intelligence engine responsible for understanding programming instructions and generating or analyzing code.

Q3. Why does Cline need to connect to Ollama?

A: Because Cline needs access to a model provider to process programming requests. Ollama supplies the locally running model through its API.

Q4. Why is Visual Studio Code the final integration stage?

A: Because VS Code hosts the Cline extension, allowing users to interact with the AI coding agent directly within their development environment.

Q5. Does this architecture require a cloud-based AI API?

A: Not necessarily. When Ollama and the model are installed locally, the core inference workflow can operate without a cloud AI API, although downloading software and models initially requires internet access.